#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167962} :activity/id #uuid "f98be8d2-8501-53a4-b5df-d4f1ac4126c5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050260 :topic/id #uuid "3a43c523-581b-58c8-a48d-f665d05c6e1f" :topic/math-academy-id 3218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168050 :tutorial/content "Recall that the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x \\in A$ and $0$ if $x \\notin A$.\n${1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]$.\n\nIn this lesson, we introduce a more general notation that allows us to write indicator functions for any condition, not just set membership.\n\nThe **Iverson bracket notation** $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ takes a predicate $P$ and returns\n${1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]P[/math] is true, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]P[/math] is false$.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[3 > 2] = 1$ because $3 > 2$ is true.\n- $\\mathbf{1}[5 \\leq 4] = 0$ because $5 \\leq 4$ is false.\n- $\\mathbf{1}[7 \\in \\{2, 5, 7\\}] = 1$ because $7$ is an element of $\\{2, 5, 7\\}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "83dcb8a1-56ef-59d0-addc-dfaa18628695" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14816 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "691d9e29-1625-5c4d-8958-88262d0bc24b" :step/math-academy-id 610829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166967 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166577 :question/id #uuid "5ebf00aa-406d-5286-b4ae-0f0044ce9b4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21905"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "144f097c-0080-5857-b992-8996f194ca22" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049002}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167300}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions to Check Predicates"} :step/id #uuid "9565a3bd-15ac-5aa0-88fe-2e6938ac1515" :step/math-academy-id 610830 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168059 :tutorial/content "The set-based notation from the previous lesson is a special case of Iverson bracket notation. Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_A(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in A]\n$$\n\nBoth expressions equal $1$ when $x \\in A$ and $0$ when $x \\notin A$.\n\nThe Iverson bracket is more flexible because it works with any predicate, such as inequalities. For example:\n\n$$\n{1\\begin{vmatrix}ifx < 0 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}ifx \\ge 0\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9a719d20-4e48-50a9-9e39-db7296bd47d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14942 :tutorial/title "Set Notation and Bracket Notation"} :step/id #uuid "c85ef936-9305-53f6-8db5-6415695e3c58" :step/math-academy-id 610833 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166968 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166579 :question/id #uuid "c831e4d2-736d-5ee1-9c0e-55cee535f361" :question/math-academy-id "e-22020"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e434dbd-b33c-5997-b3ba-043233cfbc89" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167304}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Indicator Notations"} :step/id #uuid "3c0f9089-52c4-5d6c-bd45-8b5935705854" :step/math-academy-id 610834 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168051 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions are useful for writing expressions that behave differently depending on whether a condition is met.\n\n**Sums with indicator functions.** When we multiply a term by an indicator function inside a sum, the indicator acts as a filter. For example, given values $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, the sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0] \\cdot x_i\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values. If $x_i > 0$, the indicator equals $1$ and the term contributes $x_i$. If\n\n$$\nx_i \\leq 0\n$$\n\nthe indicator equals $0$ and the term contributes nothing.\n\nConsider the values\n\n$$\nx_1 = 5, x_2 = -3, x_3 = 2\n$$\n\nUsing the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(3)1[x_{i} > 0] \\cdot x_{i} &= 1[5 > 0] \\cdot 5 + 1[- 3 > 0] \\cdot (-3) + 1[2 > 0] \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 5 + 0 \\cdot (-3) + 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 5 + 0 + 2 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0]\n$$\n\ncounts the positive values. For this data, the count is\n\n$$\n1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc59a9ec-2473-5942-9ab7-f81210d7426c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14817 :tutorial/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions: Sums"} :step/id #uuid "68e458ec-f61c-5b8a-ad94-e1c6511e3aeb" :step/math-academy-id 610837 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167968}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166969 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166578 :question/id #uuid "1687d5ff-6b48-52d9-a947-3425227af366" :question/math-academy-id "e-21906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1900018f-1bac-5623-b45f-e3d9c89f5d74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410428}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "559e173c-cc9a-5be6-9a22-191e4f99a000" :step/math-academy-id 610838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168060 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions can also express piecewise functions compactly. Consider the absolute value function:\n\n$$\n{x,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\geq 0[/math], \\\\ -x,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x < 0[/math]\n$$\n\nWe can write this as a single expression using indicator functions:\n\n$$\n\\mid x \\mid = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 0] \\cdot x + \\mathbf{1}[x < 0] \\cdot (-x)\n$$\n\nSince the conditions\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand $x < 0$ are mutually exclusive, exactly one indicator equals $1$ and the other equals $0$. This \"switch\" mechanism selects the appropriate term automatically.\n\nLet's verify this expression for a negative number, say $x = -4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid - 4 \\mid &= 1[- 4 \\ge 0] \\cdot (-4) + 1[- 4 < 0] \\cdot (- (-4)) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot (-4) + 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50ebf85-232e-5ea7-aed0-188bbb2467bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14963 :tutorial/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions: Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "ec8855d0-be42-5c66-a902-f6d870071621" :step/math-academy-id 611696 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167967}}] :activity/title "Indicator Functions for Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167001}]}
